"""Hold the captured output of TA-Lib's sixty-one `CDL` functions, or say none was captured.

Nothing in this repository imports TA-Lib. The block between the two sentinels below is
produced once, in a throwaway environment, by `scripts/capture_talib_pattern_signals.py`,
which rewrites exactly that block and leaves every word of this file alone. Running the
suite, in continuous integration or anywhere else, needs neither the library nor that
environment.

Whether a capture has happened is stated, not inferred. `_CAPTURED` is a flag the generator
writes, because "captured and TA-Lib matched nothing anywhere" and "nobody has asked
TA-Lib yet" are different facts that produce the same empty tables. The first is a finding
worth investigating; the second is a gap. Tests that need values skip on the flag and never
on a table being empty.

Everything is keyed by regime first. `series.py` builds a bundle of markets rather than one
market, and a signal only means something beside the bars it came from, so the outer key of
`SIGNALS`, `SERIES_FINGERPRINTS`, and `BAR_COUNTS` is the regime's name throughout. A
capture that covered five regimes and a bundle that defines seven then differ visibly
instead of comparing two regimes' worth of our output against nothing.

Within a regime the signals are stored sparsely. A `CDL` function returns an integer per
bar and zero on nearly all of them, so only the non-zero bars are kept and an absent index
means zero. `BAR_COUNTS` is recorded beside them, so a capture cut short cannot pass for a
series with a long tail of zeros.

`SERIES_FINGERPRINTS` is what makes the values falsifiable. Each entry is
`series.series_fingerprint()` of that regime as it stood when the capture ran, so a later
edit to `series.py` — another length, another seed, one changed constant — leaves those
signals describing bars that no longer exist, and the suite rejects them rather than
comparing our output against another series' answers.

`FUNCTION_PARAMETERS` records what each `CDL` function was called with, which for seven of
them includes a `penetration` argument carrying a library default. **It is written down,
not adopted.** Our depths come from §7 and the sources behind it; knowing what TA-Lib used
explains a disagreement and never settles one.

Why the generated block is not reformatted
------------------------------------------

The block sits between `# fmt: off` and `# fmt: on` and its lines run past the line length
the rest of the repository keeps to, which `pyproject.toml` excuses for this file alone.
Left to the formatter, twenty-one thousand bars of signals across seven regimes expand to
tens of thousands of lines of bare integers. One line per `CDL` function keeps the file
readable as what it is — a table of functions and the bars each fired on — and keeps a
re-capture's diff to the functions whose output actually moved.

Section numbers in this file are the candlestick pattern standard's,
`docs/references/candlestick_pattern_calc_spec.md`. The indicator standard numbers its
own sections separately and they do not correspond.
"""

